**Scaling Failures**

2:32 Now, let's say your app gets featured somewhere and 10,000 people show up in one business day. 2:38 That one server may not be able to handle this much traffic and even if it does not crash, the app can become painfully slow. 2:45 But let's see some of the specific ways it can fail. 2:47 And every one of these failures has a name and a fix. 2:52 That list of fixes is basically the whole field of system design.

*   **Failure #1: CPU and RAM exhaustion.** 2:54 The server runs out of CPU and RAM. 2:59 Every visitor opening their feed means your CPU is resizing images and running database queries at the same time. 3:05 Pages that loaded in 200 milliseconds now take 10 seconds. 3:08 The obvious fix is that you add a second server. 3:10 So half the people go to one machine and half go to the other. 3:14 But that creates a brand new question. 3:16 When someone opens your site, which of the two servers should they talk to, first or second? 3:24 You need a traffic officer standing in front deciding where each request goes. 3:28 That traffic officer is called a load balancer. 3:30 As the name says, it balances load on your servers, and it's the first box we'll add to our system.

*   **Failure #2: Database Overloaded.** 3:37 The database gets overloaded. 3:40 Most of those 10,000 visitors are asking for the same things, the same trending photos, the same popular profiles again and again. 3:47 And your database runs the same query from scratch every single time. 3:53 The easy fix is to keep the popular answers sitting in separate memory so that we don't have to query database again and again. 3:59 This fast memory layer is called a cache. 4:02 And this is the second box we'll add to our system.

*   **Failure #3: The Machine Dies.** 4:06 The machine just dies. 4:09 Probably a disc fails or a power supply gives out. 4:11 Someone unplugs the wrong cable. 4:14 And because everything lived on that one box, your app is down and every photo anyone uploaded could be gone. 4:21 There's a name for this problem. 4:23 It is called a single point of failure. 4:25 The fix is to keep data on more than one machine. 4:28 You can create a replica or copy of the database which is running on a different machine and your actual photo files can move to external object storage like S3 which is built to store large files safely and keep multiple copies automatically.